      "text": "To find the only integer whose square is less than its double, let's denote the integer by \\( x \\). We need to solve the inequality:\n\n\\[ x^2 < 2x \\]\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the inequality:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 2x < 0 \\]\n\nNext, we can factor out \\( x \\) from the left side of the inequality:\n\n\\[ x(x - 2) < 0 \\]\n\nThis inequality holds when \\( x \\) is between 0 and 2, but not including 0 and 2. Therefore, the only integer that satisfies this condition is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTo verify, we can check the square of 1 and its double:\n\n\\[ 1^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\]\n\nSince \\( 1 < 2 \\), the solution is correct.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{1}\\)",
